a missile that can be launched from a  train or a hypersonic one that can evade  defenses these are some of the newest  weapons in north korea's arsenal which  is already stocked with nuclear bombs  and intercontinental ballistic missiles  that could reach anywhere on the us  mainland so what they're investing in is  to make these missiles as creative and  surprising as possible  it's all part of north korean leader kim  jong-un's promise to build an invincible  military  and the growing number of weapons is  drawing attention from the u.s and its  allies in the region it's all part of a  strategy for north korea to show that  despite years of sanctions and the  struggles of kovid that at least in  terms of military it remains strong  so here's a rundown of some of the  latest technology in north korea's  arsenal and what message its expansion  sends to the world  north korean weapons tests have taken  place under all three generations of the  kim family leadership the country  launched its first homegrown missile in  1984 but the frequency of the tests  spiked under kim jong-un and included  one of the largest ever launched by the  country  tests slowed during the pandemic but in  september of this year north korea  unveiled a new weapon that would be the  first in a quick succession of tests  most recently we've seen things like  long-range cruise missiles melissa  hanham researches weapons of mass  destruction at stanford university's  center for international security and  cooperation she's been closely watching  the activity in east asia for 15 years  they're almost like a small airplane  that can turn corners fly  through valleys and most importantly fly  beneath and around radar  hanham says this quality makes a  long-range cruise missile different from  a ballistic missile which can often  carry a heavier payload and is typically  shot into space with a rocket then falls  in a faster but more predictable  trajectory  the cruise missile on the other hand can  evade detection and stealthily hit a  target  south korea itself has been building up  very powerful hyunmu cruise missiles  some of which they have touted  historically could hit kim jong-un so in  a way  north korea is probably trying to keep  pace with the missiles coming out of  south korea  about two weeks after the launch of the  long-range cruise missile north korea  said it tested another new weapon  zero  it goes much much faster than a cruise  missile that's why they emphasize  hypersonic  hypersonic missiles generally can fly a  mile per second traveling about five  times the speed of sound it can maneuver  and then dart down through the  atmosphere again in such a way that it  would be very hard for ballistic missile  defenses to track and then intercept  and so it's a way of overwhelming and  beating us ballistic missile defenses  here you can see the hypersonic missile  on display while kim jong-un surveyed  his expanding arsenal in october  south korea's military said that the  apparent hypersonic missile appears to  be in an early stage of development but  hanhem says the regime will continue  working on more nimble missiles that can  carry nuclear warheads  north korea is thought to only have a  few warheads and that means that they  need to find creative ways to deliver  these warheads overseas  and kim jong-un has declared that they  will have a multitude of ways of  delivering these  that also means having missile  deployment systems and the regime  scientists have also been working on new  projects  in september state media released  footage of two short-range ballistic  missiles being launched from a train  the idea behind it isn't really that  it's a more powerful missile or faster  missile the idea is that it's a sneaky  missile and that's thanks to the  country's extensive railway network  which stretches from the demilitarized  zone that separates the two koreas  through the country's mountainous  terrain and all the way to its border  with china and russia the reason they've  chosen rail cars it becomes nearly  impossible for the u.s and its allies to  track which rail cars have missiles and  which ones don't  another way to surprise rivals go  underwater while some marine launches  are not new pyongyang said its latest  test in october showcased a missile with  advanced features like gliding skip  mobility  a submarine gives them one more creative  way of moving around and using up  intelligence resources as  the united states and its allies guess  where these missiles are  but it isn't known if north korea fired  the missile from an actual submarine or  from a submerged barge as pyongyang is  believed to have done in earlier tests  there's a big difference between testing  this type of technology and then have it  be reliably operational or useful in a  military context  north korean analysts say the recent  military muscle flexing still sends a  strong message  north korea and kim jong-un are still  walking this fine line they're not doing  something so extreme like a nuclear test  or an icbm launch that really would draw  the wrath of the entire world  but it has the effect of just reminding  the us and south korea in particular  that we're not going to stop until we  get recognition  in the international world  the biden administration has said it's  willing to hold talks any time with  pyongyang but in a speech at the weapons  exhibit kim said the u.s couldn't be  trusted  so if and when talks do re-emerge with  south korea or the u.s they can promise  to stop doing these types of tests in  return for some type of goody be it  sanctions relief or humanitarian  assistance or  a peace declaration but these weapons  tests beyond sharpening north korea's  ability to defend itself and perhaps  intimidate u.s allies in the region it  also gives them a chip at the bargaining  table
 

